          "id": "guided-practice",
          "title": "Guided practice",
          "html": "<ol><li><strong>(15 min) Element audit.</strong> Take yesterday's five designs. For each, write which element is doing the most work — line, shape, space, texture, value or type. One line each. You'll discover that most local design relies almost entirely on photo + type, and that space is usually the missing element.</li><li><strong>(20 min) Reverse-engineer three designs.</strong> Pick three varied pieces (a logo, a poster, an app screen). For each, in your log: the element doing the most work, the Gestalt principle keeping it organised, and how value is used (is there a clear dark/light structure or is it all mid-grey?).</li><li><strong>(15 min) The greyscale check.</strong> Recreate one of those three designs' <em>hierarchy</em> in Canva in black/white/grey only. Then place the original beside it. Note what the original's colour is adding — and whether it's supporting the hierarchy or fighting it.</li><li><strong>(15 min) Negative space exercise.</strong> Design a 1080×1080 post with a single short phrase (\"Closed Sundays\", \"New in\", your own name) and nothing else. Your only tools: one typeface, two sizes, and space. It must look intentional, not empty. Save it.</li><li><strong>(10 min) Break the grouping.</strong> Take your Day 1 structural sketch. Deliberately move one caption so it sits equidistant between two blocks. Screenshot. Then fix it with spacing only. Note in one line how your eye changed.</li><li><strong>(10 min) One-element study.</strong> In Ibis Paint or on paper, make 12 thumbnails (each 20 seconds) using only circles — varying size, overlap, position. Circle three that feel like they could become logos. This is how marks are born.</li></ol>"
