          "html": "<p><strong>The clinic leaflet.</strong> A small health clinic needs an A5 leaflet explaining how to take a course of antibiotics correctly. The content they sent is 700 words of dense medical prose in long paragraphs, including dosages, side effects, when to return, and the clinic's hours.</p>\n<p>The audience: patients collecting medication at the counter, some reading in a second language, some with limited literacy, many in a hurry, standing up, in poor light. The leaflet will be printed in one colour (black) on white A5, then also posted as a photo on the clinic's Facebook page.</p>\n<p>Produce a one-page leaflet:</p>\n<ul><li>Cut the 700 words to <strong>no more than 220</strong> without losing the four things a patient must know. Write the cut text first, in a note, before designing.</li><li>Set a hierarchy that answers: <em>what is this · what do I do · how often · when to come back</em>.</li><li>Use numbers as a design element (1, 2, 3 steps) rather than a paragraph.</li><li>Include one icon or simple shape per step, drawn in Canva from primitives. No clip-art.</li><li>One colour only. Prove it survives greyscale by definition.</li><li>Leave the clinic's name, phone number and hours in a clear block at the bottom — the footer is functional, not decorative.</li></ul>\n<p>Then produce the social version: same hierarchy, square, type scaled up so it reads at thumbnail size in a feed.</p>"
