          "html": "<p><strong>\"A lion holding a laptop.\"</strong> A client runs a computer training centre and wants a logo of a lion holding a laptop, with a graduation cap, and the words \"KNOWLEDGE IS POWER\" underneath, plus the initials, in gold, \"so it looks premium\". He's seen it in his head, he's drawn it badly, and he is not asking for your opinion.</p>\n<p>Produce a <strong>written response and one alternative concept</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li>Acknowledge what he's after: strength, technology and education — write that back to him in one sentence so he knows you listened.</li><li>Explain, without condescension, the three reproduction failures (a lion + laptop + cap + tagline at 16 px, in one colour, embroidered on a polo shirt).</li><li>Recommend a concrete alternative: choose a mark type, describe the two or three core shapes, and say what it signals.</li><li>Offer the compromise that keeps the relationship: a <strong>combination mark</strong> where the lion is a simplified monogram or abstract form, and the cap appears in the identity system rather than the logo.</li><li>Keep it under 150 words. Clients read short.</li></ul>\n<p>Then build your recommended concept in Canva and put it on the test sheet, so the conversation can be held with pictures.</p>"
