          "html": "<p><strong>Three touchpoints, no laptop.</strong> Your capstone client (or a stand-in business) needs the identity applied to three real surfaces you cannot control:</p>\n<ul><li>A <strong>WhatsApp business profile picture</strong> — 500×500 px, will be seen at 60 px in a chat list, on both light and dark chat backgrounds.</li><li>A <strong>banner for the shop front</strong> — 2 m × 0.6 m, printed by a sign-writer from your file, read from 8 metres, in one colour plus white.</li><li>A <strong>receipt footer</strong> — thermal printer, black only, 58 mm wide, at 203 dpi, printed on cheap paper that smudges.</li></ul>\n<p>Produce all three as Canva designs at the correct dimensions (for the banner, work at 1:10 scale: 200 × 60 mm at 300 dpi) and add a short note for each:</p>\n<ul><li>Which lockup you used and why.</li><li>What you had to change for that medium (contrast, size, spacing, simplification).</li><li>What would break if the client used the primary lockup instead.</li></ul>\n<p>This is the exercise that proves you understand media, not just marks.</p>"
