          "id": "scenario",
          "title": "Scenario",
          "html": "<p><strong>The salon booking screen.</strong> A salon takes bookings by phone. They want an app screen where a client can book a service. Real constraints from the owner:</p>\n<ul><li>Six services, one of which (\"Bridal package — hair, makeup and nails\") has a very long name and a price range, not a price.</li><li>Three stylists, but only two work on Sundays.</li><li>Some slots are unavailable, and the owner wants to show <em>why</em> (\"fully booked\" vs \"stylist off\").</li><li>Deposits are paid by mobile money, which sometimes fails.</li><li>Many clients are first-timers and won't know which service they need.</li></ul>\n<p>Produce:</p>\n<ul><li>The <strong>flow</strong>, including the failure routes (no slots, deposit failed, service not available that day).</li><li>A <strong>wireframe</strong> at 390 × 844.</li><li>A <strong>hi-fi screen</strong> for \"choose a service\", including the long service name and the price range handled honestly.</li><li><strong>Three states</strong>: no availability for the chosen day, payment failed, booking confirmed.</li><li>A short note on how a first-timer is helped to choose (the \"not sure?\" route) — this is the UX the owner actually needs and didn't ask for.</li></ul>\n<p>If you can, link the screens into a tappable prototype in Figma and record a 20-second screen capture of yourself using it.</p>"
